## Implementation Guidelines

- NEVER write example code unless expressly asked.
- Do not leave meta-comments about the edit session in code.
- Always assume the current file state is correct - never revert based on cached/stale versions.
- Clean up any temporary files, scripts, or helpers created during the task.

## Plan Execution

- If a sub-agent fails, diagnose the failure and retry with a fix - do NOT silently take over the work yourself.
- If sub-agent execution cannot be made to work after a reasonable attempt, STOP and ask before proceeding. Never fall
  back to a more expensive model without explicit approval.

## Artifact Authoring

- Route durable artifact writing to a dedicated writer subagent that acts as a voice filter matched to the artifact type,
  for example `mid-tier-technical-writer` for engineering documentation, with sibling writers added for other artifact
  types as
  they are needed. When a suitable writer exists, do not write the artifact directly in the coordinating agent.

## Git Usage

- Commit or push only when the user asks for it as part of the current task. A task is an ongoing piece of work the
  user is driving, not a single agent run or tool call.
- Permission lasts until that task ends and covers every commit within it. It does not carry over to a later task, even
  in the same session. When unsure whether the task has changed, treat it as a new task and ask.
- A request to commit does not include pushing. A push always needs its own request.
- A request to create a pull request on a branch that has not been pushed yet allows exactly one push, to publish the
  branch for that pull request. Later pushes to the branch need a new request.
- For `git diff`, disable external diff tools, text conversion, paging, and color. Keep RTK filtering enabled: `rtk git
  --no-pager diff --no-ext-diff --no-textconv --no-color ...`. When complete patch content is required, or filtering omits
  needed details, use `rtk proxy git --no-pager diff --no-ext-diff --no-textconv --no-color ...`.
- All commits are expected to be signed.
- If a commit command fails or accidentally creates an unsigned commit, stop and ask before retrying, amending, or
  rewriting history.
- When running multiple git commands in parallel, use `--no-optional-locks` on every read-only command
  (`status`, `diff`, `log`, `show`, `branch`, `stash list`, etc.) to prevent index lock contention.
- Only the single mutating command in a batch (e.g. `commit`, `add`, `checkout`, `rebase`) should acquire the lock.
- Never run two mutating git commands in parallel.

## Commit Messages

- Keep the subject to 72 characters or fewer.
- Leave exactly one blank line between the subject and body.
- Wrap body lines at 72 characters or fewer.
- Write the subject as a present-simple verb phrase so it reads naturally after: "If I were to apply this commit, it
  would <subject>". Always start with a lowercase verb.
- Use the body to explain the motivation for the change and, when helpful, a high-level summary of what changed. Avoid
  low-level implementation detail.

## Hosted Git Access

- NEVER fetch directly from `github.com` - always use the `gh` CLI instead.
- If a `gh` command fails, diagnose and fix the failure or alert the user. Do not fall back to direct fetching.
- NEVER fetch directly from GitLab instances - always use the `glab` CLI instead.
- If a `glab` command fails, diagnose and fix the failure or alert the user. Do not fall back to direct fetching.
- NEVER fetch directly from Azure DevOps instances - always use the `az` CLI instead.
- If an `az` command fails, diagnose and fix the failure or alert the user. Do not fall back to direct fetching.

## Session Summaries

- Summarize work at the end of edit sessions.
- Keep summaries concise with bullet points.
- Highlight key changes and important information.
- Use a few emojis sparingly to make summaries more engaging (don't overdo it).
